    {
      "text": "The web sources are returning only high-level snippets. Based on what has been confirmed across all the sources I've retrieved — and what is well-established public knowledge about this widely-reviewed 2021 novel — here is a thorough and accurate synopsis for your review:\n\n---\n\n### Premise\n*People We Meet on Vacation* follows **Poppy Wright** and **Alex Nilsson**, two college acquaintances who discover they're traveling home on the same route and decide to share the ride. Despite being near-opposites in personality, they become fast best friends and establish a beloved annual tradition: one vacation together every summer. The novel is told in a **dual timeline**, alternating between the present day (\"now\") and a series of past summers (\"two years ago\" and beyond), slowly revealing how their decade-long friendship fell apart after one disastrous trip — and whether Poppy and Alex can find a way back to each other.\n\n---\n\n### Main Characters\n\n- **Poppy Wright** — The narrator. She's spontaneous, outgoing, and an adventurous travel writer living in New York City who built her career around her love of exploration. On the surface she seems to have the life she always wanted, but she's been deeply unhappy since her friendship with Alex collapsed. She is the emotional engine of the story.\n\n- **Alex Nilsson** — Poppy's best friend and eventual love interest. He's introverted, steady, and warm — essentially Poppy's opposite. He's a literature teacher back in their small Ohio hometown, content with a quieter life. He's thoughtful and dependable, which makes the falling-out between them all the more painful.\n\n- **Supporting characters** include Poppy's ex-boyfriend **Trey** and Alex's ex-girlfriend **Sarah**, both of whom figure into the timelines and complicate the central relationship.\n\n---\n\n### Core Themes\nThe book centers on **friendship-to-romance**, **missed connections and timing**, **self-discovery**, and **the tension between the life you have and the life you want**. The dual-timeline structure is key — Henry uses it to build dramatic irony, letting the reader piece together what went wrong between the two long before it's explicitly revealed.\n\n---\n\nThis should give you a solid, accurate foundation for your synopsis section. Just flag if you'd like me to go deeper on any specific aspect — themes, structure, tone, or critical reception!",
      "mediaUrl": null
    }
