      "text": "I want to be transparent with you here before reaching for any retrieval tools.\n\n**The concern:** *People We Meet on Vacation* is a commercially published novel from 2021 (Berkley/Penguin Random House) and is under active copyright. Reproducing substantial portions of the opening chapter — enough to do a meaningful craft analysis of tone, style, and voice — would go beyond what's appropriate, regardless of whether you own a personal copy. The fact that you have your own copy is great, but that doesn't change what I should reproduce here.\n\n**What I already retrieved** in the earlier tool call was a small passage (a few sentences from the opening), and that's roughly the limit of what I can responsibly surface from a contemporary copyrighted novel for this purpose.\n\n---\n\n### What I'd suggest instead:\n\n1. **Use your own copy directly** — since you have it, the opening chapter is genuinely the best source. For a craft analysis of narrative voice, you'll want to be quoting the text in your review anyway, so working from the physical book protects your accuracy and gives you quotable material.\n\n2. **I can help you *analyze* the passage you retrieved earlier.** That brief opening excerpt actually contains several strong craft signals worth discussing:\n   - The **intimate, conversational register** (\"Alex drove. Alex always drove.\")\n   - The use of **repetition and short declarative sentences** for emotional emphasis\n   - The way Poppy **editorializes in real time** (\"It is the single most rewarding sound in the world.\")\n   - The **retrospective framing** that establishes unreliable emotional distance (\"I have been chasing it ever since.\")\n\n3. **I can help you build your analytical framing** — characterizing Henry's narrative voice in craft terms (e.g., confessional, self-aware, nostalgic-with-irony) — so you have a vocabulary and structure for your review section, which you can then anchor with your own cited passages.\n\nWould you like me to take that direction? I'm glad to help you develop the craft analysis itself — I just want to make sure I'm doing it responsibly.",
